#[Description(<<<'MARKDOWN'
    Start a WordPress site migration

    Accepts the source site URL and how to reach it, stores the secret (wp-admin password or plugin token) encrypted on the migration record, and queues the migration job. method wp_admin (default) logs into the source wp-admin with username and password and installs the migrator plugin there; login_url names a custom login page. method manual_plugin takes no credentials: the user installs the plugin from POST /wordpress/migrations/plugin-package and source_token is its token. source_ip reaches the source at that public address with its own Host, e.g. while its DNS already points elsewhere. Check the same body first with POST /wordpress/migrations/test and pass the `domain` it answers: without `domain` a start takes it from `url`, and once the job has found the site's real home it moves to that home's domain (without www.) when it differs and is free, and fails when it is taken. Returns 202 with the record id and status pending. The response never includes the password or migrator token. The migration runs asynchronously in a chain of bounded queue jobs and can take from minutes to several hours on a large site. Poll the get endpoint every 1-2 minutes until status is completed, failed or cancelled; do not start a second migration for the same site while this one is running. The site keeps its own domain: a new empty classic project is created on it with no tunnel (or the existing `project` is used), the source sends the transfer to this engine's IPv4 whatever the domain's DNS says, and with PanelAlpha Connect a preview tunnel shows the site until the DNS points here. Then the engine issues the certificate and removes the preview by itself. Every call to the source plugin's transfer routes carries the import's own token. A transfer already running on the source that this engine did not start is never taken over: remote_sync fails naming it, and a resume after it has finished goes on. An engine whose default IPv4 is not public, and not mapped to a public address in the IPv4 NAT map, answers 409 engine_address_private and stores nothing, since the source could not send the transfer to it; EGRESS_ALLOW_PRIVATE lifts that for a source on the same private network.
